  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "w4FVq9R9RjbtI9IHLViq1HX6sXM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:44:03.274Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`@powerhousedao/design-system` contains \"base and scoped (project) components, utilities, and hooks for the powerhouse org\" \u2014 and its one hard setup requirement is a font.\n\nThe design system uses the **Inter** font family and does not ship it. A consumer picks one of two approaches:\n\n**Option 1 (recommended) \u2014 HTML link tags** in `<head>`, with preconnects for performance:\n\n```html\n<link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com\" />\n<link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://fonts.gstatic.com\" crossorigin />\n<link href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:ital,opsz,wght@0,14..32,100..900;1,14..32,100..900&display=swap\" rel=\"stylesheet\" />\n```\n\n**Option 2 \u2014 a CSS `@import`**, described as the fallback.\n\nThe distinction matters more than it looks: a CSS import blocks on the stylesheet before the font request begins, which is exactly the render delay the preconnect pair avoids. And the variable-font axes in that URL (`opsz`, `wght` ranges) are what the design system's typography scale expects \u2014 substituting a static Inter weight silently changes the rendering.\n\nThe \"base and scoped\" split is the other thing to know: the package carries both the neutral primitives and project-scoped components, which is why an editor importing from it can pick up Connect-specific styling.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/design-system/README.md` \u2014 intro; Installation & Setup \u203a Font Loading (Required), Options 1 and 2\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:44:03.273Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:44:03.274Z"
    }
  },
